cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use corona3d in your work, please cite it, together with the model run, the coronagraph data, and the methods that it lists for each layer."
title: "corona3d: a three-dimensional reconstruction of a coronal mass ejection and the corona from public MHD, coronagraph, and radio data"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Aggarwal
    given-names: Keshav
    affiliation: "Space Physics Laboratory (SPL), Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre (VSSC), ISRO"
    email: keshavaggarwal2310@gmail.com
version: 0.1.0
license: MIT
date-released: 2026-10-27
abstract: >-
  corona3d is a physics-based Python package that reconstructs the solar corona, a coronal mass
  ejection (CME), and the sources of its radio burst in three dimensions for any event from public
  data. It renders the electron-density surface of a PSI MAS/MAST magnetohydrodynamic model at a
  chosen plasma level coloured by the Alfven speed, traces the open and closed coronal field lines
  through the whole model with its own fourth-order Runge-Kutta tracer, and selects without free
  parameters the closed lines that pass through the CME shell, using PyVista and VTK. The graduated
  cylindrical shell of the CME is fitted automatically to coronagraph images with PyThea, with the
  intersection over union in each instrument as its objective and stated quality, guards against
  degenerate shells and unconstrained directions, and an apex height at the time of the burst
  extrapolated from the height-time profile of the front with its uncertainty. The white-light
  images of SOHO/LASCO C2 and of the STEREO COR1 and COR2 coronagraphs are drawn as ratio or
  difference planes in the plane of the sky of their spacecraft. The sources of a radio burst are
  measured in images of the Nancay Radioheliograph and of the Owens Valley Radio Observatory Long
  Wavelength Array as coloured blobs and contours, which are placed at their Newkirk heights on the
  side of the plane of the sky that faces the observer. Each panel is drawn with an orthographic
  projection from Earth, STEREO-A or STEREO-B, the solar north pole, or any chosen direction, every
  layer can be switched on or off and exported with its provenance, and a command-line interface
  driven by one configuration file reconstructs an event from its time and flare position and
  writes a report that separates what was measured from what was assumed. The CME of 2013 October
  25 is the validation case, in which the result is compared with the earlier reconstruction of
  Morosan et al. (2022), and the CME and type II burst of 2024 May 9 is a new application.
repository-code: "https://github.com/jovian-explorer/corona3d"
url: "https://jovian-explorer.github.io/software.html#corona3d"
keywords:
  - solar corona
  - coronal mass ejections
  - MHD
  - Alfven speed
  - type II radio bursts
  - LASCO
  - STEREO
  - GCS
